# Two asset classes, two policies (fast-static-site §6).
# Later rules override earlier ones, so the generic rule goes first.
#
# There is no `Vary: Accept-Encoding` here any more. Cloudflare adds it to every
# response it compresses, so the header was a line saying what the edge already
# says — and a hand-written `Vary` on a rule this broad is the kind of thing that
# quietly splits a cache key if it ever falls out of step with the edge's own.

#
# Two headers below are about transport and content rather than caching.
#
# `Strict-Transport-Security` is a year, on this hostname, and deliberately
# without `includeSubDomains` or `preload`. Both of those are promises about
# every name under smartagents.be — a mail host, a staging box, whatever n8n
# runs on — and this repo cannot see any of them. Add them the day somebody has
# checked that list; until then the apex and www are what the site is served
# from and what this covers.
#
# `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` is the real one, one step short of
# enforcing. It reports and blocks nothing, which is how a policy is rolled out:
# the browser console names anything the list below has missed, and the header
# becomes `Content-Security-Policy` when a week of real traffic has named
# nothing. There is no `report-uri`, on purpose — a reporting endpoint is a
# third-party request on a site whose whole policy is not to make any, so the
# console is the destination.
#
# What is in it and why:
#   'self'                      everything the site loads is its own.
#   style-src 'unsafe-inline'   the critical CSS is an inline <style> block in
#                               every page, and `clipDefs()` carries one inline
#                               style attribute. Both are ours, both are static.
#   'inline-speculation-rules'  the prefetch rules in every <head>.
#   challenges.cloudflare.com   Turnstile: its script, the frame it opens and
#                               the verification it posts. The one third party
#                               the site has, loaded only on interaction.
#   form-action 'self' mailto:  the contact form's no-JS fallback submits to a
#                               `mailto:` action. Without `mailto:` here, the
#                               one path that works with JS off is the one path
#                               the policy would break.
#   frame-ancestors 'none'      the same thing X-Frame-Options says, in the
#                               header that modern browsers actually read.
#
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'inline-speculation-rules' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; worker-src 'self'; form-action 'self' mailto:; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Content-hashed by Vite, so the URL changes whenever the bytes do.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Media and downloads (video, posters, one-pagers) are not content-hashed:
# revalidate daily.
/media/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# The two course one-pagers each name the page they belong to. A PDF has no
# <head> to carry a canonical, and Google reads one from a `Link` header for
# exactly that case. It is a signal, not an order: Google uses a canonical to
# choose one URL among duplicate or similar ones, a one-page fiche is not a
# duplicate of its course page, and Google may well ignore it. It stays because
# it costs nothing and names the page to prefer if a crawler does take the two
# for one. Both fiches are written in Dutch, so both point at the Dutch page:
# the agentic engineering one at the kata page it links to, the business one at
# the training page that offers it. Cloudflare adds these to the `/media/*` rule
# above rather than replacing it.
/media/SmartAgents_Agentic_Engineering_Onepager.pdf
  Link: <https://smartagents.be/nl/training/agentic-engineering-kata/>; rel="canonical"

/media/SmartAgents_AI_Business_Teams_Onepager.pdf
  Link: <https://smartagents.be/nl/training/>; rel="canonical"

# The service worker must never be served stale.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Behind a password, so nothing between the reader and the origin may hold a
# copy: `private` keeps it out of any shared cache and `no-store` keeps it out
# of the browser's own disk. The Function in `functions/secured/[[path]].js`
# says the same thing on the responses it builds itself, because a redirect it
# constructs never passes through this file.
#
# The gated area gets a policy of its own, and the difference is one word.
# `/secured/` is a separate build with separate conventions: the two Smart Scan
# documents and the decks were authored as standalone HTML and carry inline
# `<script>` blocks, and one of them an inline `onclick`. Under the site's own
# policy every one of those is a violation, so a single global rule would fill
# the console with reports about the one area that is not public and teach
# whoever reads them to ignore it. Scoped, the public site's policy stays a
# short list of things that really should never appear there, and this one says
# exactly what the gated area is still doing. Moving those scripts into files is
# what would let this line become the one above it.
/secured/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow
  Cache-Control: private, no-store
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/favicon-96.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# The project's own `pages.dev` hostnames serve the whole site as well, and to a
# crawler a second host is a second copy of the site. This is Cloudflare's
# documented pair: the first line is the production alias, the second every
# preview and branch alias under it. A placeholder in a host stops at a period,
# so neither line can match smartagents.be.
https://:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

https://:version.:project.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
